Third-party components

This repository vendors third-party code. Each component keeps its own licence
alongside the artefact it covers; none is relicensed by the MIT grant above.

Everything listed here is committed to git and compiled from source, so it is
present in any checkout. Native libraries that are *fetched and built* rather
than vendored — blend2d (lib/vyto/gfx) and ICU (lib/vyto/intl) — are not in
this repository at all and carry their own terms wherever you obtain them.

  lib/vyto/regex/native/src/pcre2/   PCRE2 10.45, by Philip Hazel and the
                                     University of Cambridge.
                                     BSD-3-Clause WITH PCRE2-exception.
                                     Bundles sljit, by Zoltan Herczeg
                                     (BSD-2-Clause), under deps/sljit/.
                                     Full text: lib/vyto/regex/LICENSE-PCRE2.txt
                                     and native/src/pcre2/LICENCE.md

  lib/vyto/crypto/ecc/native/src/    micro-ecc v1.1, by Kenneth MacKay.
    microecc/                        BSD-2-Clause.
                                     Full text: that directory's LICENSE.txt

  lib/vyto/db/sqlite/native/src/     SQLite 3.53.4 (amalgamation), by
    sqlite3/                         D. Richard Hipp and contributors.
                                     Public domain — dedicated by its authors,
                                     with no licence conditions attached.
                                     The dedication is in sqlite3.c's header.

  lib/vyto/media/image/native/src/   stb_image_write v1.16, by Sean Barrett.
    stb/                             Dual-licensed: MIT OR public domain
                                     (Unlicense), at your option.
                                     Full text: the tail of stb_image_write.h

  lib/vyto/gfx/assets/*.ttf          Inter, by The Inter Project Authors.
                                     SIL Open Font License 1.1.
                                     Full text: lib/vyto/gfx/assets/OFL.txt
                                     (apps carry copies of these files, each
                                     with the same OFL.txt beside it)

None of the above is copyleft, and none imposes a reciprocal obligation on
programs built with Vyto. The BSD and MIT terms require that their copyright
notice and permission text travel with redistributions, which is what this
file and the per-component licence files provide.
